# MySQL Workbench model. A zip archive — never let git treat it as text: a line-ending
# rewrite or an auto-merge corrupts the file silently, and Workbench then refuses to open
# it. Binary also means conflicts surface as "take mine / take theirs", which is the only
# sane resolution for a model nobody can merge by hand.
*.mwb binary

# Shell scripts must keep LF endings in the WORKING COPY, not just in the repo.
# The blobs are already LF — but core.autocrlf=true checks them out as CRLF on Windows,
# and this system is deployed by COPYING THE WORKING FOLDER to the Linux server rather
# than cloning it (deployment-guide.md §1.1). So the CRLF the developer never sees is
# exactly what lands on the server, where bash rejects it with `bad interpreter:
# /bin/bash^M` — an error that reads as a missing file or a broken shebang rather than
# as a line-ending problem. `ops/db-backup/dump-mysql.sh` and
# `ops/offsite-backup/sync-offsite.sh` run nightly, so the failure would surface as
# backups quietly not happening. A clone on Linux was never affected, which is why this
# would not reproduce anywhere except the one path we actually deploy through.
*.sh text eol=lf
